00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.598
It wasn't picking up the phone and going,
I'm gonna

00:03:29.598 --> 00:03:31.806
be the CEO of a public company, I was
picking up

00:03:31.806 --> 00:03:33.559
the phone that I was gonna do what I was

00:03:33.559 --> 00:03:36.410
passionate about and I'm going to be able
to take chances.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.768
And so I think through the course of my
career

00:03:38.768 --> 00:03:42.010
I think that people now have made it you
know.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:43.170
Kind of a joke, okay what is she

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.160
gonna do next, because its gonna be
something different.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.146
But, you know, it was, you know, leaving
Tommy Hilfiger

00:03:49.146 --> 00:03:51.775
to go run Chaps Ralph Lauren when that was
the hottest

00:03:51.775 --> 00:03:54.284
company but I knew that there were gonna
be very few

00:03:54.284 --> 00:03:57.776
opportunities for a woman president in the
men's wear industry or.

00:03:57.776 --> 00:03:58.276
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.203
&gt;&gt; Deciding to leave a company when I
didn't feel the values of the

00:04:03.203 --> 00:04:07.914
CEO of that company lined up with me and I
quit without having another job.

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:12.110
Which made Ralph Lauren come and hire me
cuz I'd been running another business.

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:14.920
It was making a decision to start up Polo
Jeans.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.379
It was making a decision to leave Polo
Jeans and

00:04:17.379 --> 00:04:20.216
go to Nike which was also very
unconventional cuz I was

00:04:20.216 --> 00:04:22.738
one of the first outsiders to really, come
in to

00:04:22.738 --> 00:04:27.430
the company, especially you know, the
fashion doyenne from New York.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:32.590
And then fast forward to how I made a
decision to, to go to HSN.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:38.120
You know I'd been at Nike for six years,
and it was a spectacular experience.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.410
I worship at the altar.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:40.880
Phil Knight he's an amazing man.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.250
And I had great opportunities at that
company,

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.900
I was the most senior woman, in the
organization.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.694
I ran their global apparel business, but I
also had the opportunity to

00:04:50.694 --> 00:04:53.009
spearhead kind of their transformation to

00:04:53.009 --> 00:04:55.760
really be more emotionally connected to
women.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.630
Some of the work on the Nike foundation
and the girl effect.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.010
But I was at a pivotal point in my career.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.273
I was in my late 40s and they had just

00:05:04.273 --> 00:05:07.414
announced a new CEO, a new president, both
of whom

00:05:07.414 --> 00:05:10.043
were about 50 and had been at the company
a

00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:14.011
long time and, most importantly they were
in very good health.

00:05:14.011 --> 00:05:18.588
So as that succession plan wasn't looking
like it was happening, [LAUGH] so fast

00:05:18.588 --> 00:05:22.460
but it was truly like you know, what was
life after Nike gonna be like?

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.280
Where was I gonna go?

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:27.343
And I think, you know, too many people
were like she's gonna go run

00:05:27.343 --> 00:05:31.335
the Gap or she's gonna go run this, and I
just knew that wasn't the

00:05:31.335 --> 00:05:36.043
case and I had the opportunity to travel
the world and really see how technology

00:05:36.043 --> 00:05:40.840
was changing the landscape, much more so
yet than it had in the United States.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:45.910
So really the explosion of mobile in Japan
and what was happening.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:50.200
You know around all the marketing efforts,
whether it be the Olympics or World Cup.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:55.550
And, I said if I gonna do anything I want
to be in a direct to consumer business.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:59.303
Because I'd worked with all these great
story telling brands and we would create

00:05:59.303 --> 00:06:01.123
the narrative around the product and we

00:06:01.123 --> 00:06:03.680
would create the technology, for example
at Nike.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:05.430
And we'd have all these stories.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.379
And then I would see my dreams dashed.

00:06:07.379 --> 00:06:07.533
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:09.554
&gt;&gt; By going onto a selling floor and
nobody

00:06:09.554 --> 00:06:12.260
was telling the story the right way to the
customer.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:15.450
So I said I wanted direct-to-consumer
business.

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:18.061
I want a business that's gonna be able to
take advantage of

00:06:18.061 --> 00:06:22.130
what's happening in technology and what's
happening in changes in consumer behavior.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:25.120
Cuz the way consumers were consuming,
right?

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.310
Media, information how people were
changing their habits of shopping.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.970
How could I take advantage of that?

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:37.450
And so when I got this call from Barry
Diller, to say I got a call from

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:40.590
a recruiter that, that Barry Diller wants
to talk

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:44.060
to you about going to take over IAC
Retail.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:46.170
And I said that's great, but what the hell
is IAC Retail?

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:47.970
I never even heard of it.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:49.760
And they said well, it's, you know, HSN

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.980
and this portfolio of these catalog and
e-commerce brands.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.600
Some of those I had heard.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:59.515
And I said, well, you know not really sure
but [UNKNOWN] wanna have lunch with

00:06:59.515 --> 00:07:03.979
[UNKNOWN] but if I wanna have lunch with
[UNKNOWN] you better have a point of view.

00:07:03.979 --> 00:07:05.437
So give me two weeks let me do all my

00:07:05.437 --> 00:07:09.440
due dilligence, let me see if this is
something really interesting.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.696
So, I completely immersed myself in the
Business and the, the

00:07:13.696 --> 00:07:18.920
brand and the competition in the market
place and I'll never forget.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.890
I was, I'm watching and I'm glued, and I
wasn't even familiar

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:26.090
with it before, I'm glued to this channel,
you know, like constantly.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.415
And my husband's like, oh my God, are we
watching this again?

00:07:28.415 --> 00:07:31.750
[LAUGH] And I also was a big Food Network
freak, right?

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.270
I love Food Network, so this was eight
years ago.

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:37.670
And I'm going, what would I do with this,
what would I do with this?

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:40.350
How can I make it more engaging and
interesting.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:40.510
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:42.726
&gt;&gt; And all of a sudden, I was watching

00:07:42.726 --> 00:07:45.938
Food Network and I clicked over to HSN and
Wolfgang

00:07:45.938 --> 00:07:48.768
Puck, who is the only person in this
video,

00:07:48.768 --> 00:07:51.620
that was on HSN at that time eight years
ago.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:54.300
None of those other people had been
launched.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.020
And the light bulb went off, and I said,
we

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:59.210
have to stop thinking of this as a selling
channel.

00:07:59.210 --> 00:07:59.270
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:00.886
&gt;&gt; And we have to start thinking of it

00:08:00.886 --> 00:08:03.560
as a network that gonna inspire people to
want things.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:04.940
I started getting really excited.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:07.680
Well we could be like HGTV and Food
Network and

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:12.600
Style and DIY and we can transport people
through experiences.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:17.280
And that's when I had lunch with Barry and
I said, okay, here's the big idea.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.940
If you are willing to buy init this idea,

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:23.054
I am willing to take this plunge and do
this.

00:08:23.054 --> 00:08:25.783
And if you are not, it's not really
interesting in

00:08:25.783 --> 00:08:30.430
great Barry fashion, because he optimise
that, he'll say go for.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:35.440
Now, little did I know, yes I was the
eightieth CEO in ten years.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:37.000
The business is stalled.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.335
It was broken.

00:08:38.335 --> 00:08:41.133
But you know, I think that sometimes the

00:08:41.133 --> 00:08:45.945
biggest challenges actually transport you
to the greatest places.

00:08:45.945 --> 00:08:47.070
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:48.885
So you decided to join HSN as CEO.

00:08:48.885 --> 00:08:49.410
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:52.440
&gt;&gt; In the first 90 days what was

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.248
your top priority when you walked into the
business?

00:08:55.248 --> 00:08:58.907
&gt;&gt; So you know, I, I knew that, when you
have

00:08:58.907 --> 00:09:05.020
eightieth CEO's in ten years, that's so
disruptive to any culture.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:06.900
And, what happens is, the culture freezes.

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:08.355
I always use the expression, it's like

00:09:08.355 --> 00:09:10.320
Mrs. Havesham in Great Expectations, you
know?

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.010
There's cobb webs, you know?

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.170
People are just waiting for the next
person to go.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:17.296
So, no strategy has been implemented.

00:09:17.296 --> 00:09:22.070
The business wasn't performing so, people
were really feeling downtrodden.

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:24.810
What were their opportunities gonna be for
success?

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:28.540
They were trailing, number two against the
competition.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:30.960
And, so, you know, I knew that I was gonna

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:35.590
have to make people feel that something
was very, very different.

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:39.569
So, my head of HR actually called me and
said and I had not visited the

00:09:39.569 --> 00:09:41.919
campus before I took the job, because they

00:09:41.919 --> 00:09:45.480
were making all these changes before I
came in.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.530
And she said, well how do you want me
arrange for your first day?

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:51.081
And I said let me ask you a question,

00:09:51.081 --> 00:09:54.750
what does any other employee do on their
first day?

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:58.220
And she said well they go to employee
orientation.

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:00.380
And she said, really?

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:03.981
I said absolutely, cuz it's gonna give me
an insight, and

00:10:03.981 --> 00:10:07.780
I think it's gonna make people feel that
it is different.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:08.150
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:10.360
&gt;&gt; So there I am, my first day, I go into
this

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:11.560
room and there's, like, 25 people,

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:15.520
and everybody goes around and introduces
themselves.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:17.090
And somebody goes well I'm a merchandising

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:19.670
assistant, I'm in backstage television,
I'm in

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:23.985
call center, and they get to me and I say
I'm the new CEO.

00:10:23.985 --> 00:10:25.268
[LAUGH] And you could see.

00:10:25.268 --> 00:10:27.520
Well that [UNKNOWN] down went to
orientation.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.300
I was in the call center, I was in
backstage

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:33.780
television, I was going to creative, I was
understanding the shows.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:35.710
I actually answered calls.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:38.320
Well what happened, it went viral.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:41.490
It went viral throughout the entire
organisation.

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:45.810
So by the next day when I got up and did
my first town hall.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.060
A, it humanized me.

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:54.650
B, it gave me context of how to articulate
what it was we needed to do.

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:54.745
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:10:54.745 --> 00:11:01.340
&gt;&gt; And it made people that I was going to
be accessible and authentic.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:04.469
And so when I got up the next morning, and
I, I said,

00:11:04.469 --> 00:11:09.950
here's why I joined the company, and we
were talking earlier about story telling.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:13.265
I knew the first story I had to tell was
why was I there, why

00:11:13.265 --> 00:11:18.690
was I passionate about the future this
company, and I had to make it personal.

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.548
But because I had that experience I was
able to make it personal.

00:11:21.548 --> 00:11:22.590
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:27.540
&gt;&gt; So all of a sudden people felt, that it
is different.

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.570
We do have a future.

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:34.181
And we're gonna, we're gonna go with her,
you know, on this path.

00:11:34.181 --> 00:11:34.394
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:11:34.394 --> 00:11:39.002
&gt;&gt; And I think that really igniting the
culture and being believable

00:11:39.002 --> 00:11:44.040
and then setting a vision is what changed
the entire business dynamic.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:46.883
&gt;&gt; You, you've talked a lot about the

00:11:46.883 --> 00:11:50.350
importance of culture, and resetting the
culture at HSN.

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:52.440
A lot of leaders talk about the importance
of culture.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:56.277
I'm wondering if you can pull back the
curtain a bit for us and tactically, apart

00:11:56.277 --> 00:11:58.874
from town halls and telling your personal
narrative,

00:11:58.874 --> 00:12:00.840
how did you build the culture at HSN to-.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:01.130
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:02.180
&gt;&gt; What it is today?

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:04.070
&gt;&gt; So the way I think about culture.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:07.574
IS I've 6,000 employees, every one of them
go home

00:12:07.574 --> 00:12:10.859
at night, every one of them has neighbors,
every one

00:12:10.859 --> 00:12:13.779
of them [UNKNOWN] visit and somebody or
they meet a

00:12:13.779 --> 00:12:16.774
new person and somebody goes who do you
work for?

00:12:16.774 --> 00:12:19.900
You want that person to be your greatest
evangelist.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:21.938
You want them to be proud of what they're
doing.

00:12:21.938 --> 00:12:22.065
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:12:22.065 --> 00:12:24.840
&gt;&gt; And they're embedding, themselves in a
culture.

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:27.283
And very often when people come to visit
at HSN,

00:12:27.283 --> 00:12:30.060
they say: oh, my God, it's almost like a
cult.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:32.500
I think that's a good thing by the way, in
a good thing, not a bad one.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:33.270
But so's Nike.

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:33.900
So's Ralph.

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:37.190
So you know, what I had to do was

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:40.670
make employee engagement the number one
priority of the company.

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:44.030
As, while, I was working on the brand and
the business strategy.

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:47.259
So with my executive leadership team, I
said we're

00:12:47.259 --> 00:12:50.910
gonna make employee engagement our, our
number one strategy.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:52.790
The second thing I did, was I was made

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:56.400
it very clear to the organization what my
expectations were.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.190
And there were three sort of people.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:01.998
There were the evangelists, that were
like, woo-hoo, we finally

00:13:01.998 --> 00:13:04.710
have someone here who is gonna like take
us somewhere.

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:08.740
The second group of people were like,
sounds interesting, but let's see.

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:13.040
You know, they needed to give it time to,
you know, get through the period.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:16.040
And the third people were the blockers.

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:18.650
They're the toxins, right?

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:23.170
I believe that you have to get rid of
toxicity in any company that you have.

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:25.500
I don't care how smart, I don't care how
talented.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.390
I don't care how they, long they've been
there.

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:30.830
If somebody is going to create a toxic
environment you have to make a change.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:32.620
Well they made it very clear to people.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:33.810
Here are my expectations.

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:36.630
I think the other thing that's important.

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:40.410
I think too many CEOs who go into
companies that are not performing

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:43.420
make the incorrect assumption that
everyone

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:45.600
in the company is probably not talented.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.810
It's usually because the leadership was

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:50.400
not inspired and it wasn't great
leadership.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.600
So what I had to do was do a talent
assessment.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:54.980
And I had a lot of talented people, that

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:58.530
were actually keeping the company together
throughout this time.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:13:58.715
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:13:58.715 --> 00:14:00.947
&gt;&gt; So I have the same, my CFO, who is now

00:14:00.947 --> 00:14:04.560
CFO and COO, Judy Schmeling has been there
for 19 years.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.320
She's been my partner throughout this
whole thing.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:09.720
I've had the same head of HR, I've had the
same operational people.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:13.290
But I did have to bring new people in to
the front of the business who were gonna

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:16.310
have to understand what a lifestyle brand
was and

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:19.040
understand kind of what we were trying to
accomplish.

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:19.930
So I had to bring in a new head of

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:21.870
television, I had to bring in a new head
of creative.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:25.310
I had to make the company become a digital
company et cetera.

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:29.892
But the number one thing I had to do was
inspire people

00:14:29.892 --> 00:14:33.454
around a vision, and I think when I'm
always asked about what

00:14:33.454 --> 00:14:37.428
a quality of leadership, that ability to
set that vision, and bring

00:14:37.428 --> 00:14:41.940
people along with you and inspire them to
get there is critical.

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:42.335
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:14:42.335 --> 00:14:47.856
&gt;&gt; So, what I did was six months after I
was there I set a giant tent up.

00:14:47.856 --> 00:14:50.980
We have a 70 acre campus in Florida.

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:54.558
So we have seven television studios, and,
it's huge.

00:14:54.558 --> 00:14:57.812
And so I erected this giant tent, and I
had 2500

00:14:57.812 --> 00:15:02.746
people, and then I broadcasted to our
other facilities outside of Florida.

00:15:02.746 --> 00:15:07.082
And we did a multimedia presentation, and
we rolled out the new vision.

00:15:07.082 --> 00:15:10.884
The mission, the strategy, we wrote a
Manifesto

00:15:10.884 --> 00:15:14.595
to our customer, and we basically told the
story

00:15:14.595 --> 00:15:21.310
of what our brand was going to be, in the
future and how we were gonna get there.

00:15:21.310 --> 00:15:25.790
And at the time, when we showed the video
at, at that event.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:28.416
We had none of these personalities, so we
had

00:15:28.416 --> 00:15:31.740
to use other people's creative to get us
there.

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:34.834
But, that was what launched all our new
creative, our

00:15:34.834 --> 00:15:37.726
mission which was to bring the joy and
excitement of

00:15:37.726 --> 00:15:41.022
new discoveries every day to our customer,
and our vision

00:15:41.022 --> 00:15:45.360
for being a disruptive force and changing
the landscape of retail.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:48.953
And they were very bold statements at the
time considering where the

00:15:48.953 --> 00:15:53.210
business was, but if you know, I think too
many people marginalize things.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:56.270
You know my husband who's an economist and
a physicist, physicist.

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:57.320
He uses this expression.

00:15:57.320 --> 00:16:02.630
Too many people are guilty of infinitesimo
incrementalism, right?

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:05.100
You do have to set both goals, but then

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:07.200
you have to give people the path to get
them.

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:09.530
You just can't put them out there without
the strategy.

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:15.160
So I think it was a combination of making
everybody feel.

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:16.880
They didn't have a job, they had a
mission.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:22.048
And if they embraced that mission and
became evangelical in a way, that we were

00:16:22.048 --> 00:16:24.757
gonna create something that didn't exist,
and we

00:16:24.757 --> 00:16:28.080
were gonna change the face of the
business.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:31.240
And that's what excites people to this
day.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:35.365
When I have those lunches, and I go why
are you here and why do you stay?

00:16:35.365 --> 00:16:40.391
The people, the culture

00:16:40.391 --> 00:16:46.130
and that we are connected to our customer.

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:48.980
We're doing something different everyday.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:53.023
The company has an intellectual curiosity,
and we're

00:16:53.023 --> 00:16:56.940
innovating and we wanna be a company of
firsts.

00:16:56.940 --> 00:16:57.000
&gt;&gt; Mm.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:57.640
&gt;&gt; That's what they say.

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:02.410
&gt;&gt; This past year you've had a pretty big
innovation in your brand.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:08.340
So you've reconcepted from, there's no
place like HSN, to, it's fun here.

00:17:08.340 --> 00:17:11.340
Tell us, we know that brand is a lot time
than a tag line.

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:13.350
How do you reinvent a brand?

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:15.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you know, it's interesting.

00:17:15.580 --> 00:17:18.350
I think that there are, are, are
different.

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:21.820
Iterations you kinda go through.

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:25.860
So when I first came into the company, it
truly was transformation.

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:28.820
I literally had to change the

00:17:28.820 --> 00:17:34.130
way everyone, employees, the marketplace,
our customer.

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:37.967
When we went public, the shareholders, how
they thought

00:17:37.967 --> 00:17:40.627
of us as a company, that we were a content

00:17:40.627 --> 00:17:44.621
company versus a selling or shopping
company, that you

00:17:44.621 --> 00:17:48.640
know, we were an innovation company, all
of those things.

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:51.272
So, that was transformational, and we
built

00:17:51.272 --> 00:17:55.130
on the brand strategy, and the business
strategy.

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:59.150
Well, we're, that, a year, year and a half
ago, we were

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:03.430
like, okay, we now need to unlock that
next level of growth.

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:05.320
And we're gonna have to be a little
disruptive.

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:06.880
And I'm a big believer if you do not

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:09.970
disrupt yourself, you're going to be
disrupted by somebody else.

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:12.120
And I said, we need to disrupt our
business a little.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:15.150
We have to reorganize how we're
structured.

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:18.860
We have to re-deploy assets against the
things that are gonna be more important.

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:21.937
It's not just watching a live show on TV,
we're gonna have to

00:18:21.937 --> 00:18:25.620
change how we create content for digital,
and how we think of ourselves.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:28.512
We're gonna have to change how we
innovate, and we're gonna

00:18:28.512 --> 00:18:31.690
have to look for strategic partners and
how we're gonna get there.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:34.542
And we're going to have to make some
massive bets

00:18:34.542 --> 00:18:39.460
on capital expenditures around systems and
supply chain, et cetera.

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:41.953
But on the brand side, what I said was,

00:18:41.953 --> 00:18:47.490
you know, we've, we've, we've articulated
there's no place like.

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:50.840
But now we have to answer the question
why.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:51.820
Now we have to tell her why.

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:56.030
We told her this is unique, now we have to
tell her why.

00:18:56.030 --> 00:19:01.610
And, so we, we did a lot of consumer work.

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:03.460
We met with a lot of our customers.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:06.980
We under, you know we do all the time.

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:08.470
We work with two different companies.

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:11.970
We work with an insights company and then
we worked with

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:16.570
Doetsch to both say, what are the things
that she's feeling?

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:20.500
But what are the things that we have to do
to be able to bring a new

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:23.490
generation of customers in without
alienating our core

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:26.390
customer, how are we going to surprise and
delight?

00:19:26.390 --> 00:19:29.493
So, we took all that work and we just, we
came

00:19:29.493 --> 00:19:34.270
up with, the answer to that question is
it's fun here.

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:35.260
It's spontaneous.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:40.290
You never know what's gonna happen next,
as I said our store changes everyday.

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:43.986
At least once a day, one of our team is
saying, you can't make this

00:19:43.986 --> 00:19:49.540
stuff up, cuz you gotta remember, it's
live 24 hours a day, it's like vagous.

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:54.170
Right, you are there at 2 o'clock in the
morning, we are pumping

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:59.131
the oxygen in and you don't know what time
[LAUGH] and that's really

00:19:59.131 --> 00:20:03.513
where, what we are about, and you know,
one of the things that

00:20:03.513 --> 00:20:08.859
had really meaning to me, and why I really
believed in this [UNKNOWN] line.

00:20:08.859 --> 00:20:11.470
You know, everybody remembers 2008, 2009
was pretty

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:13.499
[UNKNOWN] times and we [UNKNOWN] public in
August

00:20:13.499 --> 00:20:15.528
2008 probably one of the last IPL
[UNKNOWN]

00:20:15.528 --> 00:20:18.290
happened I'll never forget I spent the
summer of.

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:22.785
You know, it seemed like a good idea in
November 2007 by the way,

00:20:22.785 --> 00:20:27.880
but Judy and I spent the summer raising
the bank financing or raising the debt.

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:29.719
So, imagine two women going into all

00:20:29.719 --> 00:20:32.332
these financial institution explaining to
them why

00:20:32.332 --> 00:20:36.125
they needed to invest in a company that
was at that time all about shopping.

00:20:36.125 --> 00:20:40.208
But, we were determined and we raised
every penny and more, and

00:20:40.208 --> 00:20:43.686
we did go public, our stock came out at
about $10 and

00:20:43.686 --> 00:20:47.997
in December 2008 it was $43 at our market
cap was smaller

00:20:47.997 --> 00:20:52.708
than our [UNKNOWN] and had nothing to do
with the fundamentals of business.

00:20:52.708 --> 00:20:56.350
It had to do with the fact that the world
was falling apart.

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:59.370
And I needed to keep the organization
forward, moving forward.

00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:02.940
It's probably one of the toughest
leadership times I ever had, because I

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:06.110
knew I had the fate of 6,000 people and
their families at stake.

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:08.740
I also knew our strategy was working.

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:12.190
So we really had to go out and focus on
the sell side.

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:14.880
But I also had to focus on my customer.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:19.010
Because what I was feeling, and what my
employees were feeling, she was feeling.

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:21.745
The world was falling apart right around
her, and

00:21:21.745 --> 00:21:24.544
I remember saying to our organization, she
may not

00:21:24.544 --> 00:21:26.952
be able to buy anything right now, but
there

00:21:26.952 --> 00:21:29.740
is never gonna be any bad news on HSN.

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:34.160
We're not gonna not acknowledge what's
happening, but you know what?

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:36.520
Instead of selling high end jewelry, we're
gonna tell her

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:38.690
how to save money and cook at home for her
family.

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:42.390
We're gonna tell her why this heater is
gonna save her a fuel bill.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:46.990
Oh, we're just gonna make her feel great,
if she wants to buy a mascara.

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:48.320
And we're really going to respect her and

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:51.340
make her feel great, if she can't buy
anything.

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:54.175
This is a place that she can come and be a
respite for the

00:21:54.175 --> 00:21:59.120
storm, because it is fun here, and a lot
of people weren't having fun then.

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:03.440
And I think that our approach, both in our
employees.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:07.317
And, I, ol, the second thing about that
time, I think the companies

00:22:07.317 --> 00:22:11.329
who have really not just survived but have
thrived post that period, are

00:22:11.329 --> 00:22:15.341
the companies that decided to take it as
an opportunity to be offensive,

00:22:15.341 --> 00:22:17.908
and not defensive, and invest
appropriately

00:22:17.908 --> 00:22:22.250
and redeploy appropriately, and galvanize
against something.

00:22:22.250 --> 00:22:26.323
I'm a big believer that, how we manage
that period was a

00:22:26.323 --> 00:22:31.380
big part of our success and is what our
customer, responded to.

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:34.270
And she continues to respond.

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:36.400
And that's why we measure not

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:39.390
just employee engagement but customer
engagement.

00:22:39.390 --> 00:22:45.590
And that idea of giving her experience is
every single day are so important.

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:49.930
&gt;&gt; And since you've weathered that storm
it [UNKNOWN] has seen phenomenal growth.

00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:51.922
And one thing I am curious about is as a

00:22:51.922 --> 00:22:55.845
business leader, how do you balance
initiating things to drive revenue

00:22:55.845 --> 00:22:59.897
and spend per customer, with ensuring that
you're keeping the customers

00:22:59.897 --> 00:23:04.080
best interest in mind and they're not
spending beyond their means?

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:06.233
So you can disagree with the premise, but
I think if

00:23:06.233 --> 00:23:08.550
you get, if you get really good at what
you're doing.

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:08.990
&gt;&gt; No, no, no I think.

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:11.388
No let's talk about trust, right?

00:23:11.388 --> 00:23:16.260
So, you know, trust is across all your
constituencies, right?

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:18.605
So if you fast forward today, we have a

00:23:18.605 --> 00:23:21.890
$3 billion market cap, and a stock price
of 56.

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:23.150
We're a public company.

00:23:23.150 --> 00:23:26.330
So, you know, my belief is that the

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:30.430
best companies, yes they have to execute
quarterly.

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:35.480
But you run your business for the long
term, number one that's really critical.

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:39.933
So, you talk about trust, your employees
have to have trust, your

00:23:39.933 --> 00:23:44.830
customers have to learn to have trust,
your shareholders have to have trust.

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:46.760
And it's a combination of trust and
respect.

00:23:48.445 --> 00:23:48.495
And.

00:23:48.495 --> 00:23:50.524
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:23:50.524 --> 00:23:52.960
I'm asked the question, how can, I was
asked the

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:55.870
question earlier, well you talk about
trust all the time.

00:23:55.870 --> 00:23:59.090
But, you're kinda really selling something
to someone.

00:23:59.090 --> 00:24:04.340
And my answer to that is, yes, cuz we're
hsn.com, not hsn.org.

00:24:04.340 --> 00:24:08.260
And we have to have revenue, and we have
to have profits.

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:10.470
But we have to do it the right way.

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:15.710
So, I have to have trust and respect for
my customers, so when I got to the company

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:20.740
they were selling products that weren't of
the quality that they should be.

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:27.920
They had moved 30% of our calls off shore,
so she wasn't getting the service.

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:31.989
And they respect when she called them when
she needed

00:24:31.989 --> 00:24:36.070
to be, and we were respecting her as an
individual.

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:42.200
So we completely change the dynamics and
said that we are gonna win this and i'm

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:48.320
willing to lose profits in the short term
to create an experience.

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:51.810
That was going to retain my customers in
the long term.

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:56.430
So, we actually spent more even when we
weren't performing as a business.

00:24:56.430 --> 00:25:00.170
We brought all those calls back, we
created a work at

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:03.890
home program, we added a thousand jobs in
our local communities.

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:08.584
We were, that enabled us to get a higher
level.

00:25:08.584 --> 00:25:11.285
Individual that works for us and that
program is now six

00:25:11.285 --> 00:25:14.410
years old, and has expanded and we've
written a white paper.

00:25:14.410 --> 00:25:15.680
Now other companies use it.

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:18.490
We now have some of the highest, not

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:21.550
just employee engagement scores, but
customer engagement scores.

00:25:21.550 --> 00:25:22.300
So that was one.

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:27.800
Number two, we were very, we use a very
strong filter

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:31.490
in the products, the stories, and the
storytellers we were gonna have.

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:33.930
And we made quality our number one
priority,

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:37.200
because we actually have to excite the
customer twice.

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:39.680
Once when she feels this is a product that

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:42.190
she wants, and the second, when she gets
it home.

00:25:42.190 --> 00:25:45.270
And the last thing we want to do is
disappointing someone.

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:47.590
We want her to get even more excited.

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:50.900
The third thing that we did, was we
eradicated the word selling.

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:52.950
In our vernacular.

00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:59.210
And we changed it to we need to inspire
people to want to engage with the product.

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:02.488
So, if we are exciting our main size on

00:26:02.488 --> 00:26:05.594
the air, and he is creating an incredible
demo, and

00:26:05.594 --> 00:26:08.206
showing you how you can cook your dinner
more

00:26:08.206 --> 00:26:11.730
quickly, because you are going to have the
slow cooker.

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:14.140
And you think that is great for your
family?

00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:15.939
Our job is to give ideas,

00:26:15.939 --> 00:26:21.270
give information, give education, and
hopefully entertain.

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:24.030
So, our customer can make an informed
decision.

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:27.110
An informed decision of what she can
afford.

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:29.410
An informed decision of is this product
right for her.

00:26:30.570 --> 00:26:35.220
And an informed decision, so she can then
communicate that to other people.

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:39.180
We also made it very clear that, and we
communicate that

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:42.940
if you watch any of our shows, you can
return anything.

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:44.510
You can return anything.

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:47.830
Because if it doesn't make you happy, if
it's not what you thought you wanted,

00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:52.860
we care more about you than anything else.

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:55.360
That was a huge paradigm shift.

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:56.770
For a company.

00:26:56.770 --> 00:27:01.990
And I get letters from our customers all
the time, and I think that they

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:04.580
feel like they're more family than they

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:07.930
are customers, and I think that's really
important.

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:10.057
&gt;&gt; Speaking of family, I'm wondering if we
could

00:27:10.057 --> 00:27:11.704
switch gears a bit and talk a bit about

00:27:11.704 --> 00:27:13.777
your personal life, who are the people in
your

00:27:13.777 --> 00:27:16.450
life that have inspired your sense of self
and confidence.

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:21.550
&gt;&gt; You know, I have, I'm fortunate to have
really had a few.

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:23.900
So, it's interesting.

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:27.670
My mom, got married when she was 18.

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:30.820
She couldn't finish, high school cuz her
mother got very sick,

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:34.910
My dad was 22, when they got married and
he didn't.

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:37.480
He was in the armed services.

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:41.940
And she tried for ten, 12 years, couldn't
have children.

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:45.970
And finally, when she was 30, my dad
worked nights in the produce business.

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:48.060
And his boss, who he worked for, gave

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:51.130
my parents some money, so they could adopt
me.

00:27:51.130 --> 00:27:54.040
And so, from the time I was like little,
you

00:27:54.040 --> 00:27:56.690
know, I always remember my mom's saying,
you were special.

00:27:56.690 --> 00:28:01.021
You could do anything you want, and I felt
part responsibility you know I was kind

00:28:01.021 --> 00:28:05.110
of a little serious you know, I get small
as younger to prove that I could do.

00:28:05.110 --> 00:28:07.238
But, there is one thing my mother always
said

00:28:07.238 --> 00:28:09.720
to me that has stuck with me my whole
life.

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:12.084
And it probably annoyed me sometimes at
the

00:28:12.084 --> 00:28:14.820
time which it probably does my daughter
now.

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:17.778
But, when something would happen, whether
it was good, or whether it

00:28:17.778 --> 00:28:21.310
was bad, if a boyfriend broke up with me,
or something good happen.

00:28:21.310 --> 00:28:24.031
And I was complaining, or I was excited
she said

00:28:24.031 --> 00:28:28.700
[UNKNOWN] everything in life is bechert,
means it's meant to be.

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:32.770
And what it's made me do, is, I never look
backwards.

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:33.838
I only look forward.

00:28:33.838 --> 00:28:37.423
Because it's like that, movie, movie
sliding

00:28:37.423 --> 00:28:40.309
door who knows what could happen if that

00:28:40.309 --> 00:28:43.632
bad thing didn't happen, you know, what,

00:28:43.632 --> 00:28:47.760
what, what else could've been behind the
door?

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:50.028
Which is why I believe I'm an optimist you
know, I

00:28:50.028 --> 00:28:52.350
have been quoted as I only hire [UNKNOWN]
I don't hire

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:55.158
[UNKNOWN] I'm not very good at negative
energy, but I said

00:28:55.158 --> 00:28:57.980
everything in life is meant to be, and I
really embrace that.

00:28:58.990 --> 00:29:02.170
So that's probably from a personal
perspective, and then I

00:29:02.170 --> 00:29:06.270
think from a business perspective there's
definitely been a few things.

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:12.450
Working for Ralph Lauren, you're really,
you're at the [INAUDIBLE] brand, right?

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:16.975
And I'll never forget when I was launching
a number you know, of our brands, he used

00:29:16.975 --> 00:29:19.269
to come and walk through the show room,
and

00:29:19.269 --> 00:29:21.650
he would show up it would be like
[INAUDIBLE].

00:29:21.650 --> 00:29:24.920
And he would go up to something, it could
be a fixture.

00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:28.794
Or it could be a, you know, shirt and you,
Mindy, tell

00:29:28.794 --> 00:29:33.310
me why this Ralph Lauren before I put the
label on it.

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:36.070
Where did it come from, what was it
inspired by, what's the

00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:39.160
integrity behind that product, and why
should it be part of my brand.

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:43.398
Second thing he taught me was, it's more
important

00:29:43.398 --> 00:29:46.760
what you learn to say no to in your brand.

00:29:46.760 --> 00:29:50.301
Because if you make that one mistake you
will violate the trust of

00:29:50.301 --> 00:29:53.972
what that brand is, and there have been
quite a number of, whether

00:29:53.972 --> 00:29:55.874
they be brands or celebrities, or

00:29:55.874 --> 00:29:58.498
personalities that have been an
opportunity to

00:29:58.498 --> 00:30:02.930
come to HSN, and I probably could have
done a lot of business.

00:30:02.930 --> 00:30:04.404
But, I knew they weren't right for our
brand.

00:30:04.404 --> 00:30:06.215
I knew they weren't right for our culture.

00:30:06.215 --> 00:30:08.335
I knew they weren't the right kind of
people.

00:30:08.335 --> 00:30:10.884
And sometimes you have to let business go.

00:30:10.884 --> 00:30:13.375
If it's not the right thing for your
brand.

00:30:13.375 --> 00:30:16.156
And then you know, Phil Knight who is

00:30:16.156 --> 00:30:19.495
one of the most inspirational, amazing
people in

00:30:19.495 --> 00:30:22.517
what he believes in, in terms of
inspiration

00:30:22.517 --> 00:30:26.750
and innovation and culture and humanity is
so important.

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:29.882
But the one, one thing I really remember,
I was having

00:30:29.882 --> 00:30:33.694
this conversation with him and I must have
been frustrated that one

00:30:33.694 --> 00:30:36.483
of the people on my team, you know, I'm
like, I

00:30:36.483 --> 00:30:40.720
can't get them to, you know, evolve, you
know, or move up.

00:30:40.720 --> 00:30:45.240
You know, I was trying to fix people,
right.

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:49.540
And, he said he finally said to me, Mindy,
you will be a lot more successful.

00:30:49.540 --> 00:30:51.250
Company will be a lot more successful.

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:53.610
And, you will be a lot less frustrated if
you stop

00:30:53.610 --> 00:30:55.740
trying to make ordinary people
extraordinary

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:57.750
and surround yourself with extraordinary
people.

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:00.365
And, I always learned that, you know.

00:31:00.365 --> 00:31:02.760
There's an extraordinary in everyone.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:05.190
And it doesn't matter how many degrees you
have.

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:06.980
It doesn't matter what your background is.

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:09.130
It doesn't matter what your providence is.

00:31:09.130 --> 00:31:12.060
You need to look for extraordinary people
with soul.

00:31:12.060 --> 00:31:16.090
People who have the humanistic
characteristics, as well as the talent.

00:31:17.346 --> 00:31:22.501
And then lastly, working for Barry, even
though it was only a couple of years, it's

00:31:22.501 --> 00:31:27.368
like, idea, and I was always a risk taker,
but, really to understand what that

00:31:27.368 --> 00:31:30.948
means in today's environment, that, you
know, you've

00:31:30.948 --> 00:31:33.239
gotta be able to block out the external

00:31:33.239 --> 00:31:38.290
noise, and do what you think is, is right,
even if it goes against the grain.

00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:43.780
&gt;&gt; I wanna make sure we leave some time
for questions from the audience.

00:31:43.780 --> 00:31:47.230
But before we do that, you have done a lot
of these interviews.

00:31:47.230 --> 00:31:50.414
And I have a lot of questions [LAUGH]
we're not gonna have time to get to today.

00:31:50.414 --> 00:31:53.320
I'm curious when you are done with an
interview, if you're

00:31:53.320 --> 00:31:57.230
ever left thinking, I wonder why they're
not asking me x.

00:31:57.230 --> 00:32:00.460
What should we be asking you that we're
not asking you?

00:32:02.170 --> 00:32:03.906
&gt;&gt; I have found that there's a lot of

00:32:03.906 --> 00:32:06.945
smart young people who really know what
questions to ask.

00:32:06.945 --> 00:32:11.090
[LAUGH] I, I wish some of you were at some
of our investor conferences.

00:32:11.090 --> 00:32:13.780
Because you ask much better questions.

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:19.188
You know, you know, I think too many
people,

00:32:19.188 --> 00:32:25.050
you know, ask the, the business questions.

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:27.980
I think what we talked about today is
really important.

00:32:27.980 --> 00:32:31.190
I think you, you have to ask the soul
questions.

00:32:31.190 --> 00:32:33.900
You know, what drives people, what
motivates

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:37.140
people, whether it's an employee or a
customer.

00:32:37.140 --> 00:32:41.970
And, you know, when I look at the leaders
that I respect, and

00:32:41.970 --> 00:32:46.650
I go to all these conferences, what I
wanna hear is the why.

00:32:46.650 --> 00:32:48.980
I wanna hear the why behind.

00:32:48.980 --> 00:32:50.500
You know, when I go and meet

00:32:50.500 --> 00:32:53.030
with investors, I know that they're
investing in

00:32:53.030 --> 00:32:54.860
the company, but they're also investing in

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:57.640
me and they're investing in my leadership
team.

00:32:57.640 --> 00:33:00.992
And I actually ask them to ask the tougher
questions, you know,

00:33:00.992 --> 00:33:04.440
ask the questions of, you know, why, why
do you believe this?

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:05.490
Not just the numbers.

00:33:05.490 --> 00:33:06.420
Not just the things.

00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:08.190
What are they telling you?

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:10.090
How do you look at your competition.

00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:12.941
Why do you think you're gonna be
successful?

00:33:12.941 --> 00:33:13.838
It's the whys.

00:33:13.838 --> 00:33:14.610
It's the whys.

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:18.726
And you have to keep digging deeper you
know, particularly as

00:33:18.726 --> 00:33:23.065
you're deciding, you know, what businesses
you wanna, wanna to be

00:33:23.065 --> 00:33:26.003
in, in you know, I talk to people you know
a

00:33:26.003 --> 00:33:29.490
lot and I'll say you know, what are you
looking for?

00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:31.836
What are you looking for in a company?

00:33:31.836 --> 00:33:34.353
And I get the business side of it and I'm

00:33:34.353 --> 00:33:37.172
like no, no, no what are you looking for
in a

00:33:37.172 --> 00:33:40.606
company that you think aligns with who you
are and what

00:33:40.606 --> 00:33:44.060
your values are and is it a company poised
for growth?

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:46.540
Is it a company poised for success?

00:33:46.540 --> 00:33:49.370
And I think too many people get entranced
by big.

00:33:50.480 --> 00:33:52.700
They get entranced by size.

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:56.890
They gotta get entranced by opportunity,
and what

00:33:56.890 --> 00:33:59.710
you think you can bring to the table.

00:33:59.710 --> 00:34:03.907
And that's why that conversation about
impact is so important

00:34:03.907 --> 00:34:06.994
to me because what impact was when I was
20 and

00:34:06.994 --> 00:34:10.159
what I could do, and I tried to have
impact, and

00:34:10.159 --> 00:34:14.140
I tried to make other people successful
that was my impact.

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:18.888
What I think of impact today and creating
value, what I think

00:34:18.888 --> 00:34:23.700
of impact today from it personally and
philanthropy, is a very different thing.

00:34:23.700 --> 00:34:26.948
If I've been privileged to be able to get
to this

00:34:26.948 --> 00:34:31.510
point, I have a responsibility now to have
impact beyond myself.

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:35.528
So whether it's my work at UNICEF or going
into the field,

00:34:35.528 --> 00:34:40.570
or HSN cares those are the things that are
the most rewarding.

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:42.110
So again, it goes back to the why.

00:34:43.480 --> 00:34:45.607
&gt;&gt; I have lots of questions, I'd love to

00:34:45.607 --> 00:34:48.070
ask you but, I wanna give you guys a
chance.

00:34:48.070 --> 00:34:50.477
So, we're gonna open it up for five
minutes of

00:34:50.477 --> 00:34:53.587
questions for Mindy, and then we'll close
it out of here.

00:34:53.587 --> 00:34:55.905
&gt;&gt; First question from the audience here.

00:34:55.905 --> 00:34:59.723
Twitter was Mindy, when you first arrived
at HSN, how did you

00:34:59.723 --> 00:35:04.550
identify the people who you could, trust
and rely upon for your transformation?

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:07.140
&gt;&gt; It's a great question, and it was the
first thing I needed to do.

00:35:08.660 --> 00:35:11.830
As I said before, I brought my leadership
team together,

00:35:11.830 --> 00:35:16.120
as well as articulated to the company what
my personal philosophy

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:20.730
was, what was acceptable in behavior, and
the people and

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:24.310
the character that I was looking for, and
what wasn't acceptable.

00:35:24.310 --> 00:35:29.145
We very quickly defined what the elements
of our culture were, and what people had

00:35:29.145 --> 00:35:34.270
to do and the type of people that were
going to be successful in that culture.

00:35:34.270 --> 00:35:39.600
And then I really had to put that through
a filter and determine who was

00:35:39.600 --> 00:35:45.730
not going to be successful and determine
who was and make some hard decisions.

00:35:45.730 --> 00:35:47.000
And there were hard decisions.

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:50.537
There were some people who, I will tell
you right now, have gone on

00:35:50.537 --> 00:35:54.710
you know, to other companies, but I knew
that they didn't fit the future.

00:35:54.710 --> 00:35:56.540
And they wouldn't fit with me.

00:35:56.540 --> 00:36:04.900
And they weren't going to be a positive
you know, element of this transformation.

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:06.278
And they were gonna make it harder.

00:36:06.278 --> 00:36:11.450
And you know, the, the key was is to get
in my head.

00:36:11.450 --> 00:36:14.510
I needed people who could inspire people.

00:36:14.510 --> 00:36:18.810
I needed people who understood what a
transition was to a lifestyle brand.

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:20.312
I needed people that were gonna be

00:36:20.312 --> 00:36:22.840
evangelists and work to engage the
culture.

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:26.176
That had positivity, that certainly they
had to have the brains

00:36:26.176 --> 00:36:29.326
and the intellect and the capability, but
it wasn't about a

00:36:29.326 --> 00:36:32.228
resume, it was about who those human
beings are and more

00:36:32.228 --> 00:36:36.895
important were they gonna make an ambition
and were they gonna commit.

00:36:36.895 --> 00:36:40.155
fortunately, as I mentioned before, I had
some very

00:36:40.155 --> 00:36:44.068
talented people, but what I really
appreciate is the first

00:36:44.068 --> 00:36:46.603
people I hired, you know Bill Brand is now

00:36:46.603 --> 00:36:50.970
our CMO and head of business development
for the corporation.

00:36:50.970 --> 00:36:55.640
Andy Sheldon is now opening our
entertainment offices in LA.

00:36:55.640 --> 00:36:58.370
So all the people that came in, in the
beginning,

00:36:58.370 --> 00:37:00.980
are still there because they still think
it's a mission.

00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:02.250
And I think that's really important.

00:37:09.100 --> 00:37:10.080
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Dorothy Walter.

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:10.530
I'm MBA 1.

00:37:10.530 --> 00:37:14.210
Thank you so much for coming in to speak
with us today.

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:19.406
I have a question about how you view HSN's
role in launching new brands.

00:37:19.406 --> 00:37:21.506
What are some of the success factors and
also

00:37:21.506 --> 00:37:24.520
risks, and how do you wrap your head
around that?

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:25.720
Thank you.

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:27.050
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:37:27.050 --> 00:37:32.140
I actually think of it as a privilege, to
work with new brands.

00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:37.680
And I think in today's world, a lot of
brands,

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:40.700
it gets, it's harder and harder to tell
their stories, right?

00:37:40.700 --> 00:37:42.320
Brands are distribution captive.

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:43.440
There are less stores.

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:44.970
There are less vehicles.

00:37:44.970 --> 00:37:48.780
The places you can't tell your story,
they're very crowded.

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:52.282
So, to be able to help a new brand or a
younger brand,

00:37:52.282 --> 00:37:57.120
being able to give voice to their story is
really exciting to me.

00:37:57.120 --> 00:38:00.080
So, I get as excited about the
entrepreneur

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:03.040
who created this product in her garage so
her

00:38:03.040 --> 00:38:08.960
kids could learn how to flip and fold as I
am, you know, Keith Urban and his guitar.

00:38:08.960 --> 00:38:14.990
They're all rock stars, so what we do is
spend a lot of time trying to understand

00:38:14.990 --> 00:38:20.620
what the brand wants to articulate and
what the DNA of that brand is.

00:38:20.620 --> 00:38:22.420
And, you know, the way I look at it, I

00:38:22.420 --> 00:38:25.190
have a competitor two and a half times my
size.

00:38:25.190 --> 00:38:27.470
So if it's just about how many of these
you can

00:38:27.470 --> 00:38:30.900
sell in the first five minutes, I'm not
gonna be very compelling.

00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:35.270
But if my value proposition is that I'm
gonna be able

00:38:35.270 --> 00:38:39.630
to tell your story and I'm gonna invest in
understanding your story.

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:42.545
And I'm gonna invest in articulating that
whether

00:38:42.545 --> 00:38:46.069
it's through a set, whether it's through
an environment,

00:38:46.069 --> 00:38:48.917
whether it's through a creative or
whatever that

00:38:48.917 --> 00:38:51.509
is, and I'm gonna bring your story to
life.

00:38:51.509 --> 00:38:53.240
That to me is a relationship.

00:38:54.310 --> 00:38:57.840
And we will do everything possible to make
that successful.

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:02.040
Now that is not to say that everything
works, it doesn't.

00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:04.840
I can do anything for a brand, but I can't
personally make

00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:08.940
the product sale if for some reason it's
not resonating with the customer.

00:39:10.038 --> 00:39:12.998
But we will do everything in our power,
and

00:39:12.998 --> 00:39:17.450
we will never end a relationship
disrespectfully or abruptly.

00:39:17.450 --> 00:39:21.000
You know, we will be very clear throughout
that process.

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:23.951
And we've actually had some people that we
didn't

00:39:23.951 --> 00:39:27.107
have immediate success with, but a year
later they'd

00:39:27.107 --> 00:39:30.126
honed their business or their product or
their brand

00:39:30.126 --> 00:39:33.030
and we decided to relaunch them and have
success.

00:39:34.410 --> 00:39:35.970
So what do our teams do?

00:39:35.970 --> 00:39:39.600
Our teams are scouring you know, the world

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:43.060
for great products, and great stories, and
great brands.

00:39:44.090 --> 00:39:46.230
We also have ideas, and we create them.

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:49.490
You know, what we're doing with Disney for
example, at the end of this month.

00:39:49.490 --> 00:39:51.710
We saw Oz the Great and Powerful.

00:39:51.710 --> 00:39:56.357
So we're Disney partner for Maleficent,
which is launching going

00:39:56.357 --> 00:39:59.875
into theaters at the end of March with
Angelina Jolie.

00:39:59.875 --> 00:40:03.588
So we went out to 50 different brands from
small to big

00:40:03.588 --> 00:40:09.300
and we had people create products inspired
by the inspiration of the story.

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:12.247
And we're creating a 24-hour event on air.

00:40:12.247 --> 00:40:17.510
So, you have a little entrepreneurial
brand next to, you know, a big brand.

00:40:17.510 --> 00:40:21.710
But what they're all doing is just
creating inspiring stories.

00:40:21.710 --> 00:40:24.120
And that's what's exciting for us, is to
be able to do

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:26.650
that.

00:40:26.650 --> 00:40:27.310
&gt;&gt; You wanna take.

00:40:27.310 --> 00:40:27.430
Okay.

00:40:27.430 --> 00:40:28.160
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:28.350
&gt;&gt; Oh.

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:28.570
Yeah.

00:40:28.570 --> 00:40:30.070
One last question.

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:30.740
&gt;&gt; No pressure.

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:31.286
&gt;&gt; No pressure.

00:40:31.286 --> 00:40:31.740
[LAUGH]

00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:33.714
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Gillian [INAUDIBLE] I'm
an MBA too.

00:40:33.714 --> 00:40:37.588
My question is around how your see you're
role and you're objective as a

00:40:37.588 --> 00:40:39.874
CEO today, and I'm wondering how that's

00:40:39.874 --> 00:40:42.370
changed since you took the role on
initially?

00:40:44.510 --> 00:40:50.784
&gt;&gt; So, you know, I look at the, the CEO
role as on multiple levels right?

00:40:50.784 --> 00:40:55.947
I am responsible for making sure I'm
creating sustainable

00:40:55.947 --> 00:41:00.798
value for my company, which is going to
ultimately then

00:41:00.798 --> 00:41:05.858
create value for all the constituencies we
have, whether

00:41:05.858 --> 00:41:11.040
it's our customers, our employees or our
shareholders.

00:41:12.950 --> 00:41:19.920
But I'm also responsible for being the
shepherd of my brands and for my company.

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:22.660
And I'm also responsible for creating

00:41:22.660 --> 00:41:26.640
sustainable opportunities and success for
people.

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:30.820
And I take all of that very, very
seriously.

00:41:32.580 --> 00:41:34.740
And I say all of that because sometimes,

00:41:34.740 --> 00:41:37.750
to your earlier point, they can be
conflicting priorities.

00:41:37.750 --> 00:41:41.406
There are times where, you know, I have to

00:41:41.406 --> 00:41:45.774
make a decision, and I've not made the
decision to

00:41:45.774 --> 00:41:50.547
make money in that quarter, because I'm
not gonna make

00:41:50.547 --> 00:41:56.030
the wrong decision for the long term of
the business.

00:41:56.030 --> 00:42:01.070
But I'm very clear about why I decided to
do something, and my share holders

00:42:01.070 --> 00:42:06.131
understand because I've been consistent
with our strategy and our story.

00:42:06.131 --> 00:42:09.007
For six years I've never deviated, so they

00:42:09.007 --> 00:42:12.349
trust the decisions that I'm making, even
though

00:42:12.349 --> 00:42:15.147
it might have a short term disruption, are

00:42:15.147 --> 00:42:18.190
right for the long term value of the
business.

00:42:18.190 --> 00:42:20.429
Which is why I think we have long term

00:42:20.429 --> 00:42:24.850
shareholders and great institutions
because that really is my job.

00:42:24.850 --> 00:42:28.444
Is to understand the choices and to make
the right

00:42:28.444 --> 00:42:33.100
and educated choices for the long term
success of the company.

00:42:33.100 --> 00:42:39.634
The second thing, is my job as a CEO to
really lead and be a role model

00:42:39.634 --> 00:42:45.720
for what I do and what I say, both
internally and externally.

00:42:46.790 --> 00:42:49.730
And it's to set the, an example.

00:42:49.730 --> 00:42:52.870
And I take that really seriously, because
the worst thing that I

00:42:52.870 --> 00:42:56.010
could do, is sit up here and say, we do
all of this.

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:58.560
And then all of a sudden, I act
differently.

00:42:58.560 --> 00:42:59.810
Or I say something different.

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:02.630
It's not real and it's not authentic.

00:43:04.310 --> 00:43:07.668
So I have to kind of live, and walk the
walk.

00:43:07.668 --> 00:43:12.120
And whether it be about, you know, why we
launched Davidson Cares?

00:43:12.120 --> 00:43:14.222
Or what we have to invest in, or if I

00:43:14.222 --> 00:43:17.845
do have to, you know, consolidate a
business, and maybe I

00:43:17.845 --> 00:43:21.180
do have to layoff some people, because, if
I don't,

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:25.680
I can't deploy the resources that will
take the company forward.

00:43:25.680 --> 00:43:29.380
But anyone that knows me, knows that if I
do that, I'm gonna do it respectfully.

00:43:30.910 --> 00:43:34.140
And people are gonna understand the
reasons why.

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:36.760
So I look at that role in very broad
terms.

00:43:39.400 --> 00:43:41.833
&gt;&gt; Mindy, most of us here today are
thinking

00:43:41.833 --> 00:43:44.613
about what to do next now that school is
wrapping

00:43:44.613 --> 00:43:47.392
up and charting career paths of our own
with some

00:43:47.392 --> 00:43:50.380
uncertainty, and this is a quote that you
said last

00:43:50.380 --> 00:43:53.368
year at in New York, and this is something
that

00:43:53.368 --> 00:43:56.425
I know you tell your daughter, I'm
wondering if you

00:43:56.425 --> 00:43:58.858
could expand on it in closing for us as
MBAs

00:43:58.858 --> 00:44:01.610
and how you might encourage us to leap
into it.

00:44:01.610 --> 00:44:08.730
&gt;&gt; I think it's really important that
people not be afraid of risk.

00:44:09.770 --> 00:44:13.650
Now, there is a big difference between
risk and suicide, okay.

00:44:13.650 --> 00:44:14.570
I just want to be clear.

00:44:14.570 --> 00:44:21.110
And what I mean by that is, don't only
look at what's in front of you today.

00:44:21.110 --> 00:44:24.090
Don't only look at what's out there today.

00:44:24.090 --> 00:44:28.170
Find, take the insights of everything that
you've learned.

00:44:28.170 --> 00:44:30.820
Take the insights and intellectual
curiosity

00:44:30.820 --> 00:44:32.830
you have to look what's out there.

00:44:32.830 --> 00:44:35.210
And say what do I want to be part of?

00:44:36.250 --> 00:44:41.830
What do I want, you know, what do I want
to get that experience, right?

00:44:41.830 --> 00:44:44.370
What do I see as growing?

00:44:44.370 --> 00:44:48.420
What do I see as, you know, that, that
next thing?

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:51.077
It's kind of like we're looking at a lot
of acquisitions right now and

00:44:51.077 --> 00:44:52.518
I'm looking for those things that are

00:44:52.518 --> 00:44:54.560
going to have the big growth
opportunities, right?

00:44:56.570 --> 00:44:58.559
And you have to decide what level of risk

00:44:58.559 --> 00:45:01.960
you're gonna take at any given point in
your career.

00:45:01.960 --> 00:45:03.893
But the time when you can really do it

00:45:03.893 --> 00:45:07.570
and have the different experiences is, is
early, right?

00:45:07.570 --> 00:45:13.490
I, I had a, I had a number of friends who
I said to them, you are miserable, right?

00:45:13.490 --> 00:45:14.700
Why are you still doing this?

00:45:14.700 --> 00:45:16.160
Cuz they felt trapped.

00:45:16.160 --> 00:45:18.030
Never be in a position to feel trapped.

00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:22.960
Believe in yourself enough that you need
to find those things that are.

00:45:22.960 --> 00:45:26.356
Cuz if you are inspired, and you are
excited, and you are apart

00:45:26.356 --> 00:45:28.537
of something, you are probably gonna be

00:45:28.537 --> 00:45:31.580
successful because you're doing what you
love.

00:45:31.580 --> 00:45:35.867
And you know, it's interesting, my husband
went to school, got his

00:45:35.867 --> 00:45:38.339
undergraduate, his maters and his post

00:45:38.339 --> 00:45:41.260
graduate in applied mathematics and
physics.

00:45:41.260 --> 00:45:44.070
He's like a real geek, he said he was
Steven Hawkins, right.

00:45:44.070 --> 00:45:47.030
And he goes I don't think I wanna do it,
then he went to law school,

00:45:47.030 --> 00:45:48.350
you know, and then he went, and he

00:45:48.350 --> 00:45:51.330
practiced law for five years, and he was
miserable.

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:55.990
And, this kinda went, I, I met him, and he
said you know what, I, I can't do this.

00:45:55.990 --> 00:45:59.163
And he just decided to give up law and
made a segue

00:45:59.163 --> 00:46:02.553
and said, I wanna figure out maybe how to
go into banking

00:46:02.553 --> 00:46:05.655
which he did, and he went and he said I'm
gonna make

00:46:05.655 --> 00:46:10.590
my segue from, into derivatives and swap
documentation and went into finance.

00:46:10.590 --> 00:46:11.511
&gt;&gt; He had some backup options.

00:46:11.511 --> 00:46:12.184
[LAUGH]

00:46:12.184 --> 00:46:15.352
&gt;&gt; but, but you know, he's always been one
of those people

00:46:15.352 --> 00:46:18.454
that, you know, at his true heart and soul
I think he's

00:46:18.454 --> 00:46:22.942
an academic, but he's got this incredible
intellectual curiosity and the most

00:46:22.942 --> 00:46:26.920
important thing for him is, I need to fill
my intellectual curiosity.

00:46:26.920 --> 00:46:29.740
So that was always his guiding post.

00:46:29.740 --> 00:46:32.780
You know, mine was I gotta be inspired and
I have to

00:46:32.780 --> 00:46:36.350
be creative, and I have to have an outlet
for my creativity.

00:46:36.350 --> 00:46:40.061
The most important thing you can do, and
the most empowering

00:46:40.061 --> 00:46:44.450
thing you will ever do throughout your
entire life, is know yourself.

00:46:45.830 --> 00:46:46.890
Know yourself.

00:46:46.890 --> 00:46:50.600
I think self-awareness is the key to
everything.

00:46:50.600 --> 00:46:53.890
&gt;&gt; We spend a lot of time on that [LAUGH]
here at Stanford Business School.

00:46:53.890 --> 00:46:54.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:46:54.200 --> 00:46:56.310
Know, know the good, the bad, and the
ugly.

00:46:56.310 --> 00:46:56.610
Right?

00:46:56.610 --> 00:46:56.750
Okay?

00:46:56.750 --> 00:46:57.711
&gt;&gt; You're on the right path.

00:46:57.711 --> 00:46:57.980
[LAUGH].

00:46:57.980 --> 00:47:02.770
&gt;&gt; Trust me, you know, I know what my
derailers are, right?

00:47:02.770 --> 00:47:06.164
But, it's empowering if you know what
you're made of, because then you

00:47:06.164 --> 00:47:10.610
can put those things through the filter of
what's real and what's important.

00:47:10.610 --> 00:47:13.743
And, when people work for you, if you're
really honest,

00:47:13.743 --> 00:47:17.128
and you're willing to tell people what
your strengths are, and

00:47:17.128 --> 00:47:20.513
your willing to tell them what your
weaknesses are, there gonna

00:47:20.513 --> 00:47:24.540
trust you a lot more, because your willing
to share that.

00:47:24.540 --> 00:47:26.940
And we have a joke, we all know what our
derailers are.

00:47:26.940 --> 00:47:29.450
Mine, I'm not very patient sometimes.

00:47:30.648 --> 00:47:34.270
One of the other guys is mischievous, so
he'll try and you know?

00:47:34.270 --> 00:47:36.500
When, when you're under stress, you have
to know what it is.

00:47:36.500 --> 00:47:39.370
So we can be in a meeting, and we'll be
able to call each other out.

00:47:39.370 --> 00:47:41.730
And go, you're derailing, right?

00:47:42.780 --> 00:47:47.666
And I, I just think it's, you know,
self-awareness is empowering.

00:47:47.666 --> 00:47:50.350
So know everything you can about yourself,
know

00:47:50.350 --> 00:47:53.039
what gets you excited, and then find that
path.

00:47:53.039 --> 00:47:55.726
&gt;&gt; Mindy thank you so much for being here
with me.

00:47:55.726 --> 00:47:56.518
&gt;&gt; Thank you for having me.

00:47:56.518 --> 00:47:57.364
&gt;&gt; We really appreciate it.

00:47:57.364 --> 00:48:00.244
[SOUND].

00:48:00.244 --> 00:48:01.400
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:48:01.400 --> 00:48:06.120
[MUSIC]

